package enclavid:host@0.1.0;

/// The applicant's material as the host holds it: one stored byte-blob each,
/// minted by the runtime into `event::media` and rehydrated across rounds by
/// its hash. It lives in the host package, not in `enclavid:policy`, so the
/// policy and any verification plugin hold the SAME resource type and can pass
/// a handle across the fused policy↔plugin boundary; the bytes stay host-side
/// and enter a component's linear memory only when it pulls `bytes`.
interface blob {
    /// A blob's content ADDRESS — the hex-encoded 32-byte BLAKE3 of its bytes.
    /// An opaque STRING token (NOT one of the `-ref` resources of `refs` —
    /// those are opaque per-round capability handles; this is a serializable
    /// value): the policy stashes it in its opaque sealed `state` and passes it
    /// to the `blob` constructor to rehydrate across rounds; it never inspects
    /// it. A string (not raw bytes) for cross-language ergonomics — JS/Go/Rust:
    /// JSON-safe, `==`-comparable, map-keyable, loggable (`[]byte` can't even
    /// be a Go map key). TYPE-AGNOSTIC: any kind of blob reuses the same store
    /// and hash. (Algo-agility, if ever needed, via a multihash-style `algo:`
    /// prefix inside the string.)
    type blob-hash = string;

    /// One stored byte-blob — a host-owned lazy CONTENT handle (has `bytes`),
    /// distinct from the opaque `-ref` capability handles. The universal unit of
    /// storage: 1 blob ↔ 1 `blob-hash` ↔ 1 sealed blob host-side. What a blob
    /// holds is said by the record that carries it (`enclavid:policy/types`'s
    /// `capture`) and read by the plugin it is handed to — not by this type:
    /// a photo is one JPEG, its bytes as the camera page encoded them; a video
    /// is a WebM of VP8 frames, as the page's encoder wrote them. A verification
    /// plugin pulls the bytes via `bytes` (so they enter linear memory only at
    /// use); the policy keeps the `blob-hash` in `state` to rehydrate the blob
    /// in a later round.
    resource blob {
        /// Rehydrate the blob with this content hash from the session's media
        /// store. Own-session only (cross-session is cryptographically blocked).
        /// Returns a COLD handle — no host IO yet; the bytes are pulled lazily on
        /// the first `bytes()`. TRAPS on a miss (unknown / wrong-length hash): a
        /// fabricated hash is never a legitimate outcome for a correct policy (it
        /// only ever stashes hashes it got from a real `hash()`), so it fails loud
        /// rather than handing the policy a recoverable branch. (A constructor is
        /// possible precisely because there's no error result — only blob-or-trap.)
        constructor(hash: blob-hash);
        /// Pull this blob's bytes into the caller's linear memory. For a
        /// rehydrated (constructed) blob this is the LAZY load point. Bounded by
        /// the round's memory max: a round that goes past it is ended.
        bytes: func() -> list<u8>;
        /// This blob's content hash — stash in `state` to rehydrate the blob in
        /// a later round via the constructor.
        hash: func() -> blob-hash;
    }
}
